      "content": "That's very well phrased. I think that \"convenient fiction\" line is particularly punchy.\n\nI'm curious, though—as I'm building this argument, some of my colleagues might argue that the nationalist movement in the book is actually responding to legitimate concerns about cultural identity and social cohesion, rather than just being \"manufactured.\" How would you suggest I frame my rebuttal to that? I want to show that even if their concerns feel real to them, their specific ideological conclusions are still logically unsound and ultimately harmful.",
